1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,729
Your local AI model loads successfully. Your

2
00:00:02,729 --> 00:00:05,349
GPU has enough memory. The interface

3
00:00:05,349 --> 00:00:07,417
says ready. You type a question,

4
00:00:07,417 --> 00:00:08,920
hit enter, and then you wait.

5
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:11,619
The first word takes forever. The next words

6
00:00:11,619 --> 00:00:14,215
appear slowly. And a model that looked perfectly

7
00:00:14,215 --> 00:00:17,256
reasonable on the download page suddenly feels worse

8
00:00:17,256 --> 00:00:19,360
than a much smaller one. So what happened?

9
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,317
Most people look at one number: VRAM.

10
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:26,440
Eight gigabytes. Twelve. Sixteen. Twenty-four. But once

11
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:28,988
a model fits, capacity is only

12
00:00:28,988 --> 00:00:30,780
the beginning of the performance story.

13
00:00:31,102 --> 00:00:33,554
Today, we're going to trace the actual path

14
00:00:33,554 --> 00:00:36,691
of one local AI response, locate the bottlenecks

15
00:00:36,691 --> 00:00:38,771
that make it slow, and build a testing

16
00:00:38,771 --> 00:00:42,420
method that separates real improvements from impressive-looking screenshots.

17
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:46,262
No mystery token-per-second claims. No fantasy benchmark

18
00:00:46,262 --> 00:00:48,702
bars. Just what the hardware is doing,

19
00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:50,288
what the software is doing, and

20
00:00:50,288 --> 00:00:51,920
which setting is worth checking first.

21
00:00:52,243 --> 00:00:54,226
Start with something easy to miss. Your

22
00:00:54,226 --> 00:00:56,350
local model has two very different jobs.

23
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:58,743
The first job is reading your prompt.

24
00:00:58,743 --> 00:01:00,498
If you paste a short question, that

25
00:01:00,498 --> 00:01:02,169
may be a small amount of work.

26
00:01:02,169 --> 00:01:04,188
If you paste a repository, a long

27
00:01:04,188 --> 00:01:07,541
document, and yesterday's entire conversation, it can

28
00:01:07,541 --> 00:01:09,200
be an enormous amount of work.

29
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:12,695
This stage is called prefill, or prompt processing.

30
00:01:12,695 --> 00:01:15,748
The model processes the incoming context and prepares

31
00:01:15,748 --> 00:01:17,250
the state it will need to answer.

32
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:19,948
The second job is generating the answer. This

33
00:01:19,948 --> 00:01:23,217
is called decoding. For an ordinary autoregressive language

34
00:01:23,217 --> 00:01:25,958
model, the answer comes out token by token.

35
00:01:25,958 --> 00:01:27,605
Each new token depends on what came

36
00:01:27,605 --> 00:01:30,350
before it. These are not interchangeable measurements.

37
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:33,177
A benchmark showing fast prompt processing does

38
00:01:33,177 --> 00:01:36,134
not prove fast generation. A benchmark showing

39
00:01:36,134 --> 00:01:38,586
fast generation after a tiny prompt does

40
00:01:38,586 --> 00:01:41,134
not prove your thirty-page document will feel

41
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:43,454
responsive. And a screenshot of a model

42
00:01:43,454 --> 00:01:46,090
sitting in GPU memory proves neither one.

43
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:48,742
Watch for two numbers instead. Time to first

44
00:01:48,742 --> 00:01:50,654
token tells you how long it takes before

45
00:01:50,654 --> 00:01:53,514
a response begins. Generation speed tells you

46
00:01:53,514 --> 00:01:55,810
how quickly the response continues after that.

47
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:58,251
Even time to first token is not purely

48
00:01:58,251 --> 00:02:01,485
a prefill number. It can include setup, scheduling,

49
00:02:01,485 --> 00:02:03,528
and other overhead. That is why you need

50
00:02:03,528 --> 00:02:05,930
to record the workload along with the measurement.

51
00:02:06,262 --> 00:02:08,714
Here is the first bottleneck: the GPU

52
00:02:08,714 --> 00:02:09,808
can be good at one of those

53
00:02:09,808 --> 00:02:11,930
jobs and less impressive at the other.

54
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:15,551
During a substantial prefill, the GPU often has

55
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:18,172
enough parallel work to keep its computing hardware

56
00:02:18,172 --> 00:02:22,054
busy. During single-user decoding, many dense language models

57
00:02:22,054 --> 00:02:24,181
are limited more by moving data than

58
00:02:24,181 --> 00:02:27,080
by performing arithmetic. That difference is important.

59
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,100
A graphics card's advertised compute power tells you

60
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,371
how much arithmetic it may be capable of

61
00:02:32,371 --> 00:02:34,583
under the right conditions. It does not tell

62
00:02:34,583 --> 00:02:36,770
you how quickly a particular local model can

63
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:39,559
repeatedly move its weights and working data through

64
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:42,070
memory while producing one token at a time.

65
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:46,648
Memory bandwidth matters. Kernel efficiency matters. Model architecture

66
00:02:46,648 --> 00:02:49,710
matters. The precision used to store weights matters.

67
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:52,769
Two graphics cards can both have sixteen gigabytes

68
00:02:52,769 --> 00:02:55,606
of VRAM and still behave very differently with

69
00:02:55,606 --> 00:02:57,926
the same model. They might have different memory

70
00:02:57,926 --> 00:03:01,750
bandwidth, different compute capabilities, and different software support.

71
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:03,877
And that explains why the question,

72
00:03:03,877 --> 00:03:05,656
"Does it fit?" cannot answer the

73
00:03:05,656 --> 00:03:07,380
question, "Will it be fast?"

74
00:03:07,712 --> 00:03:09,647
Here is the second bottleneck: the

75
00:03:09,647 --> 00:03:10,596
part of the model that is

76
00:03:10,596 --> 00:03:12,410
not actually running on your GPU.

77
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:14,890
Imagine you have a model that needs slightly

78
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:17,474
more working memory than your card can provide.

79
00:03:17,474 --> 00:03:20,323
Some local AI runtimes can place selected model

80
00:03:20,323 --> 00:03:23,051
layers or tensors in system RAM and compute

81
00:03:23,051 --> 00:03:24,450
part of the work on the CPU.

82
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:27,125
That can be useful. Without offloading, the

83
00:03:27,125 --> 00:03:28,844
model might not run at all. But

84
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:30,851
system RAM is not simply slower

85
00:03:30,851 --> 00:03:33,110
VRAM attached to the same processor.

86
00:03:33,432 --> 00:03:35,968
The CPU and GPU are separate computing

87
00:03:35,968 --> 00:03:39,610
devices. Their memory systems, compute performance, and

88
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:41,730
connection between them all affect the result.

89
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:44,325
If each generated token has to pass through

90
00:03:44,325 --> 00:03:48,339
CPU-resident work, your GPU may spend time waiting,

91
00:03:48,339 --> 00:03:50,200
even while its usage meter looks low.

92
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:52,400
Do not assume that every small amount of

93
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,235
CPU offloading causes the same slowdown. The penalty

94
00:03:56,235 --> 00:03:59,143
depends on the model, runtime, number and type

95
00:03:59,143 --> 00:04:02,640
of offloaded layers, CPU performance, and hardware connection.

96
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:06,188
The practical test is straightforward. Record the model's

97
00:04:06,188 --> 00:04:10,563
actual GPU-offloaded portion, not merely its downloaded size.

98
00:04:10,563 --> 00:04:13,327
Then compare like-for-like runs with a configuration

99
00:04:13,327 --> 00:04:15,570
that fits more completely on the GPU.

100
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:18,117
If the smaller model feels dramatically more

101
00:04:18,117 --> 00:04:20,076
responsive, that may tell you more

102
00:04:20,076 --> 00:04:21,580
than the parameter count ever did.

103
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:25,137
Now the third bottleneck: your conversation is growing,

104
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:27,280
even when the model file has not changed.

105
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,489
A model can start quickly with a one-line

106
00:04:29,489 --> 00:04:31,604
prompt and feel very different after a

107
00:04:31,604 --> 00:04:34,188
long working session. You add code. You

108
00:04:34,188 --> 00:04:37,217
add logs. You add retrieved documents. You

109
00:04:37,217 --> 00:04:39,780
ask follow-up questions without clearing the history.

110
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:42,278
All of that increases the amount of context

111
00:04:42,278 --> 00:04:44,645
the runtime must handle, unless some of

112
00:04:44,645 --> 00:04:46,810
it is trimmed or reused through caching.

113
00:04:47,179 --> 00:04:50,292
For many transformer architectures, the runtime stores

114
00:04:50,292 --> 00:04:53,105
attention-related key and value information in a

115
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:55,833
KV cache. The amount of memory required

116
00:04:55,833 --> 00:04:58,586
depends on the model's architecture, active sequence

117
00:04:58,586 --> 00:05:01,190
length, precision, and the cache implementation.

118
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:04,054
Longer contexts can increase memory pressure and the

119
00:05:04,054 --> 00:05:06,734
cost of processing new input. They can also

120
00:05:06,734 --> 00:05:09,810
increase the attention-related work done during generation.

121
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:13,022
But be careful with the simplified diagrams online.

122
00:05:13,022 --> 00:05:15,522
Not every modern model uses exactly the same

123
00:05:15,522 --> 00:05:18,587
attention layers or the same cache behavior. Hybrid

124
00:05:18,587 --> 00:05:22,370
architectures and alternative attention mechanisms change the picture.

125
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:24,814
What remains true is more practical than

126
00:05:24,814 --> 00:05:28,336
any universal formula: a maximum advertised context

127
00:05:28,336 --> 00:05:29,850
length is not a promise that your

128
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:32,500
GPU can use that entire window comfortably.

129
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,026
Try a controlled comparison. Use the same

130
00:05:35,026 --> 00:05:37,261
model and runtime. Ask the same kind

131
00:05:37,261 --> 00:05:39,413
of question with a short prompt, then

132
00:05:39,413 --> 00:05:41,985
with a substantially longer prompt. Record the

133
00:05:41,985 --> 00:05:44,641
delay before generation starts, the speed of

134
00:05:44,641 --> 00:05:46,910
the answer, and the GPU memory used.

135
00:05:47,230 --> 00:05:49,073
If the long prompt is the problem,

136
00:05:49,073 --> 00:05:51,249
buying a faster GPU may not be

137
00:05:51,249 --> 00:05:54,831
your first useful change. Removing irrelevant context,

138
00:05:54,831 --> 00:05:58,448
retrieving fewer unnecessary passages, or taking advantage

139
00:05:58,448 --> 00:06:00,290
of supported prompt caching may help.

140
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:02,966
That is the fourth bottleneck: the quantization

141
00:06:02,966 --> 00:06:04,560
you chose to make the model fit.

142
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:08,180
Quantization stores model weights using fewer bits.

143
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:09,779
It can let a larger model fit

144
00:06:09,779 --> 00:06:11,582
in the same memory budget. It can

145
00:06:11,582 --> 00:06:13,445
reduce memory traffic. And with a

146
00:06:13,445 --> 00:06:16,290
well-supported implementation, it can improve speed.

147
00:06:16,610 --> 00:06:18,576
But lower bit depth does not automatically

148
00:06:18,576 --> 00:06:20,632
mean higher speed. The runtime has

149
00:06:20,632 --> 00:06:22,879
to understand that format. The GPU

150
00:06:22,879 --> 00:06:25,050
backend needs efficient kernels for it.

151
00:06:25,372 --> 00:06:28,101
Some quantization schemes require additional work to

152
00:06:28,101 --> 00:06:31,382
unpack or process weights. And certain combinations

153
00:06:31,382 --> 00:06:34,014
of model, hardware, and software may be

154
00:06:34,014 --> 00:06:36,779
better optimized than others. A smaller file

155
00:06:36,779 --> 00:06:39,483
can therefore produce a disappointing result, even

156
00:06:39,483 --> 00:06:41,090
when the memory saving is real.

157
00:06:41,410 --> 00:06:43,989
There is another trade-off. More aggressive compression

158
00:06:43,989 --> 00:06:46,249
can alter model behavior. The size of

159
00:06:46,249 --> 00:06:48,532
that effect depends on the model, quantization

160
00:06:48,532 --> 00:06:50,864
method, and task. It should be tested

161
00:06:50,864 --> 00:06:52,835
on the actual work you care about,

162
00:06:52,835 --> 00:06:54,890
not inferred from a single impressive answer.

163
00:06:55,210 --> 00:06:56,811
If you are coding, use the same

164
00:06:56,811 --> 00:06:58,939
small set of coding tasks. If you

165
00:06:58,939 --> 00:07:01,691
are extracting facts, check the extracted facts

166
00:07:01,691 --> 00:07:04,179
against the source. If you are summarizing,

167
00:07:04,179 --> 00:07:06,130
inspect what was omitted or invented.

168
00:07:06,450 --> 00:07:08,236
Do not choose a quantization solely

169
00:07:08,236 --> 00:07:10,039
because it has the largest parameter

170
00:07:10,039 --> 00:07:11,840
count or the smallest download.

171
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:15,087
The fifth bottleneck is less glamorous: the software

172
00:07:15,087 --> 00:07:17,270
path between your prompt and the GPU.

173
00:07:17,590 --> 00:07:19,840
Local AI can run through several backends

174
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:22,785
and interfaces. Different runtimes support different

175
00:07:22,785 --> 00:07:26,848
optimized kernels, attention implementations, batching strategies,

176
00:07:26,848 --> 00:07:29,750
caching features, and device offloading choices.

177
00:07:30,070 --> 00:07:32,174
That is not a promise that one application

178
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:35,227
is always faster than another. Performance varies by

179
00:07:35,227 --> 00:07:39,878
hardware, model architecture, version, settings, and workload. But

180
00:07:39,878 --> 00:07:42,042
it does mean a poorly configured runtime

181
00:07:42,042 --> 00:07:43,790
can waste the hardware you already own.

182
00:07:44,110 --> 00:07:46,962
If your GPU supports an accelerated backend and

183
00:07:46,962 --> 00:07:49,774
the model is compatible, confirm the runtime is

184
00:07:49,774 --> 00:07:52,418
actually using it. Check whether layers are being

185
00:07:52,418 --> 00:07:56,180
offloaded as expected. Check the selected context limit.

186
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:58,175
Look at memory allocated after you load the

187
00:07:58,175 --> 00:08:00,440
model and again after a long prompt.

188
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:02,988
And before copying an optimization flag from

189
00:08:02,988 --> 00:08:05,284
a forum, verify that the flag applies

190
00:08:05,284 --> 00:08:08,012
to your runtime version and model architecture.

191
00:08:08,012 --> 00:08:09,803
A setting designed for one system

192
00:08:09,803 --> 00:08:11,520
is not a universal speed button.

193
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:14,563
Some optimizations, such as batching, aim to

194
00:08:14,563 --> 00:08:16,402
improve the total number of requests a

195
00:08:16,402 --> 00:08:19,250
system handles. That is throughput. Others aim

196
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:21,414
to shorten the wait for one person.

197
00:08:21,414 --> 00:08:23,680
That is latency. Those goals can conflict.

198
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,747
A server processing many users may benefit from

199
00:08:26,747 --> 00:08:29,271
continuous batching. Someone waiting for a single

200
00:08:29,271 --> 00:08:31,242
answer on a home desktop may care

201
00:08:31,242 --> 00:08:33,586
much more about time to first token

202
00:08:33,586 --> 00:08:35,910
and the pace of individual output tokens.

203
00:08:36,230 --> 00:08:38,255
So when a benchmark boasts an enormous

204
00:08:38,255 --> 00:08:42,209
tokens-per-second figure, ask one question immediately: is

205
00:08:42,209 --> 00:08:44,829
that one conversation, or the combined output

206
00:08:44,829 --> 00:08:47,305
of many conversations running at once?

207
00:08:47,305 --> 00:08:49,540
The numbers answer very different questions.

208
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:51,785
Now we have enough information to design a

209
00:08:51,785 --> 00:08:55,174
proper test. First, write down the exact GPU

210
00:08:55,174 --> 00:08:58,612
model and VRAM capacity. Not just the brand.

211
00:08:58,612 --> 00:09:01,320
Not just sixteen gigabytes. The exact card.

212
00:09:01,643 --> 00:09:03,831
Second, write down the model name, the

213
00:09:03,831 --> 00:09:07,076
exact quantization file, and the runtime version.

214
00:09:07,076 --> 00:09:08,735
If any of those change, you are

215
00:09:08,735 --> 00:09:10,840
no longer testing the same configuration.

216
00:09:11,174 --> 00:09:13,565
Third, record the context setting and the

217
00:09:13,565 --> 00:09:16,101
actual input length. A model tested with

218
00:09:16,101 --> 00:09:18,433
a paragraph cannot be fairly compared with

219
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:20,570
a model tested against a large document.

220
00:09:20,923 --> 00:09:24,024
Fourth, separate prompt processing from generation. In a

221
00:09:24,024 --> 00:09:29,120
llama.cpp-based workflow, the llama-bench utility reports prompt-processing

222
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:31,968
and text-generation tests separately. Its results are

223
00:09:31,968 --> 00:09:34,757
useful, but the tool's own documentation also

224
00:09:34,757 --> 00:09:37,090
explains what its measurements do not include.

225
00:09:37,431 --> 00:09:40,315
Fifth, repeat the test. Run more than once,

226
00:09:40,315 --> 00:09:42,827
watch the variation, and avoid treating the best

227
00:09:42,827 --> 00:09:46,121
single result as the normal experience. Also check

228
00:09:46,121 --> 00:09:48,633
whether the first run includes model loading

229
00:09:48,633 --> 00:09:51,020
or cold-start effects that later runs avoid.

230
00:09:51,372 --> 00:09:54,256
Finally, test real tasks. A synthetic speed

231
00:09:54,256 --> 00:09:55,686
figure does not tell you if the

232
00:09:55,686 --> 00:09:59,593
model correctly follows instructions, produces usable code,

233
00:09:59,593 --> 00:10:01,510
or stays coherent through a long session.

234
00:10:01,830 --> 00:10:04,637
The most honest comparison has two columns, not

235
00:10:04,637 --> 00:10:07,390
one: how well the model performs your task,

236
00:10:07,390 --> 00:10:09,100
and how comfortably your hardware runs it.

237
00:10:09,424 --> 00:10:10,818
So what should you actually change

238
00:10:10,818 --> 00:10:12,330
when your local AI feels slow?

239
00:10:12,652 --> 00:10:15,333
Start by identifying whether the delay happens before

240
00:10:15,333 --> 00:10:17,616
the first word or during the answer. If

241
00:10:17,616 --> 00:10:20,429
the first word is slow, inspect prompt length,

242
00:10:20,429 --> 00:10:23,998
prefill performance, startup overhead, and caching. If

243
00:10:23,998 --> 00:10:27,580
the answer itself crawls, inspect GPU offloading,

244
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:30,870
memory bandwidth, model size, and decoding efficiency.

245
00:10:31,191 --> 00:10:32,597
Then look at the memory budget during

246
00:10:32,597 --> 00:10:35,073
the workload, not just the idle model.

247
00:10:35,073 --> 00:10:36,864
A model that barely fits before you

248
00:10:36,864 --> 00:10:38,811
paste a long document may not be

249
00:10:38,811 --> 00:10:41,050
a sensible configuration for that document.

250
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:43,382
If a smaller quantization would free enough

251
00:10:43,382 --> 00:10:44,692
room to keep more work on the

252
00:10:44,692 --> 00:10:47,312
GPU, test it. If a smaller model

253
00:10:47,312 --> 00:10:50,028
gives better responsiveness without sacrificing the

254
00:10:50,028 --> 00:10:52,120
quality you need, test that too.

255
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:53,587
And if you are deciding whether to

256
00:10:53,587 --> 00:10:56,267
buy new hardware, measure before you spend.

257
00:10:56,267 --> 00:10:58,142
A faster GPU can help, but the

258
00:10:58,142 --> 00:11:00,010
right upgrade depends on the bottleneck.

259
00:11:00,330 --> 00:11:04,794
Additional VRAM solves capacity problems. Higher memory bandwidth

260
00:11:04,794 --> 00:11:07,907
can help memory-limited decoding. More compute can help

261
00:11:07,907 --> 00:11:10,936
compute-heavy phases. None of those phrases means

262
00:11:10,936 --> 00:11:13,180
every workload scales in the same way.

263
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,624
There is no single best GPU for

264
00:11:15,624 --> 00:11:17,655
every local AI user, because there

265
00:11:17,655 --> 00:11:19,930
is no single local AI workload.

266
00:11:20,253 --> 00:11:23,775
Someone generating short chat replies, someone summarizing huge

267
00:11:23,775 --> 00:11:26,527
documents, and someone serving multiple users are

268
00:11:26,527 --> 00:11:28,880
asking the hardware to do different jobs.

269
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,467
The best configuration is not the one with

270
00:11:31,467 --> 00:11:33,702
the biggest number in the product listing. It

271
00:11:33,702 --> 00:11:35,926
is the one that stays responsive and accurate

272
00:11:35,926 --> 00:11:37,960
at the context length you actually use.

273
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:39,982
That is the difference between a model that

274
00:11:39,982 --> 00:11:41,960
launches and a model you can work with.

275
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:43,727
And it gives us a better question to

276
00:11:43,727 --> 00:11:46,227
ask in the comments. Tell me your exact

277
00:11:46,227 --> 00:11:49,881
GPU, the model and quantization, the runtime, and

278
00:11:49,881 --> 00:11:52,321
your typical context length. Then tell me which

279
00:11:52,321 --> 00:11:54,544
part feels slow: waiting for the first

280
00:11:54,544 --> 00:11:56,510
word, or watching the answer come out.

281
00:11:56,830 --> 00:11:59,068
If enough people share those details, we can

282
00:11:59,068 --> 00:12:02,410
compare real configurations instead of competing screenshots.

283
00:12:02,730 --> 00:12:04,663
This is Byte Forensics. Measure the

284
00:12:04,663 --> 00:12:07,091
workload, find the bottleneck, and only

285
00:12:07,091 --> 00:12:09,470
then decide what to upgrade.
